WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.180
Joe Pesci is one of the all-time great and
versatile character actors.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:09.170
He played Jake Lamotta's brother and manager
in Raging Bull the psychopathic Tommy DeVito

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:14.200
in Goodfellas, and had legendary roles in
Home Alone, My Cousin Vinny, and the Lethal

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.590
Weapon franchise.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:19.619
Pesci was a welcome presence on the big screen
for decades who could successfully handle

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:21.130
high drama and humor.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.560
"But I'm funny how?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.800
I mean, funny like I'm a clown, I amuse you?"

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:28.420
But lately, we haven't heard much from the
Oscar-winner, while his talents have been

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.420
sorely missed.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:34.000
Here are some of the reasons Joe Pesci's been
off the radar in recent years.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.130
Early retirement

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:40.160
In 1999, when his acting career was still
very much alive, Pesci announced he'd largely

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.870
retire from the biz in order to pursue his
first love: music.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:48.280
Now, while a lot of actors decide to record
an album or take a role in a Broadway musical

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.500
just because they can, Pesci actually has
some chops.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.040
In the '60s, he was the guitarist for the
band Joey Dee and the Starliters, a position

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.700
he vacated to a slightly better guitarist
named Jimi Hendrix.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:05.270
In 1968, he released an album called Little
Joe Sure Can Sing! under the stage name of

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.270
Joe Ritchie.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:10.400
He even introduced the two musicians who'd
go on to form the Four Seasons — but then

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.920
his acting career took off, and music went
on the back burner for 30 years.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.240
Pesci's retirement came just after the release
of a bizarre LP called Vincent LaGuardia Gambini

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.180
Sings Just for You.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.130
Something of a novelty project, the title
is derived from the name of Pesci's My Cousin

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.130
Vinny character.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.840
Guess that whole "be a good lawyer" thing
didn't work out for ol' Vinny, huh?

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:31.840
Just for friends

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.390
Every once in awhile, Pesci pops out of retirement
for an acting gig here and there, but there's

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.170
usually a good reason for it.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.630
For example, Pesci is still very close friends
with Robert De Niro.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.580
Over the course of four decades, they've starred
in a number of movies together, among which

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.700
are the best of both actors' storied careers
like Raging Bull, Casino, and Goodfellas,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.229
the latter of which netted Pesci the Academy
Award for Best Supporting Actor and what may

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:57.930
be the shortest Oscar acceptance speech in
history.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.210
"It was my privilege.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.510
Thank you."

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:07.040
It's his bond with De Niro that led to Pesci's
only major role of note since 2000: a cameo

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.140
in The Good Shepherd, the 2006 movie that
was also Robert De Niro's directorial debut.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.300
Other than that, Pesci's only been seen in
the 2010 brothel drama Love Ranch, which combines

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.480
both boxing and the gambling scenes for which
he's famed, and a 2011 Snickers commercial

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.480
in which he played himself.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.480
"Whattya lookin at?"

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.440
"I wasn't, I mean, I'm not looking at anything."

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.480
"We're not good enough for you.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.350
You lookin' for something else?"

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.560
Losing interest

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.490
Now that we know he's semi-retired and only
comes out of hiding for a role once in awhile,

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:36.520
let's talk about why.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.580
A glance at his résumé reveals very few
movies after the classic Casino was released

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:46.040
in 1995, and what he was being offered really
couldn't compete with his best films.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.910
Remember 8 Heads in a Duffel Bag?

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:48.950
Or Gone Fishin'?

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:54.200
Pesci's last major movie was the 1998 sequel
Lethal Weapon 4, which earned him a Razzie

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:59.350
Award nomination for Worst Supporting Actor
for reprising his role as the obnoxious informant

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:00.350
Leo Getz.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:04.560
Those movies, and his subsequent walk away
from Hollywood, reflects a comment Pesci made

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.680
to the New York Times in 1992:

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:10.080
"I love to star in movies, but I want to have
good roles.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.690
It doesn't help to get starring roles in something
that's no good.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.450
I mean, that will just kill you."

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.520
Besides, how could any subsequent role possibly
compete with what he's already done?

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.180
"What the f--- is this?

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.120
Where's this f---ing guy gonna land, the fairway?"

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:27.760
Gains and losses

00:03:27.761 --> 00:03:31.879
Even after Pesci retired, he was still willing
to take on a big role if it all the factors

00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:32.879
aligned.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:37.930
Around 2011, he was set for a major supporting
role in a film about the Gambino crime family

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.660
with John Travolta attached to star as John
Gotti, and Pesci set to portray Gotti's friend

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.640
and personal "enforcer," Angelo Ruggiero.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:50.370
Ruggiero was a big guy, so Pesci dutifully
gained 30 pounds for the role.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.540
Then he was reportedly dropped into a smaller
part — and offered a reduced salary.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.970
Surely, he must have had some choice words
for the execs who came to him with that.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:04.071
"Without me, you, personally, every f---ing
wise guy still around, will take a f---ing

00:04:04.071 --> 00:04:06.310
piece of your *beep* ass!"

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:10.870
Pesci ultimately committed his ire to paper
and filed suit against the production company,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.849
ending in an undisclosed settlement in 2013.

00:04:13.849 --> 00:04:17.569
And the Gambino movie still hasn't been filmed,
with or without Pesci.

00:04:17.569 --> 00:04:21.229
But that wasn't the only legal entanglement
he's found himself in ...

00:04:21.229 --> 00:04:23.969
The ex-wife, the hitman, and the attorney

00:04:23.969 --> 00:04:29.849
Pesci was married to model-actress Claudia
Haro from 1988 to 1992, with whom he had a

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:30.849
daughter.

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:35.050
They must have remained on good terms, because
Haro's acting career started after the divorce,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.270
and of the five movies she appeared in, four
were Pesci films.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.900
Pesci also stood by her side during a very
bizarre legal matter.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:46.180
After her split from Pesci, Haro married Hollywood
stuntman Garrett Warren.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:51.360
But things soured in 1999…and about a year
later, Warren was shot by a stranger at the

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.100
front door of his home in Westlake Village,
California.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:58.300
It took years to figure out who shot him,
but some evidence uncovered in the trunk of

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:03.039
a car in a drug bust — directions to Warren's
house and his photograph — set police off

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:08.020
and running, and they eventually figured out
that Haro had paid the assailant, a hitman,

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.639
to kill her ex-husband.

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:12.509
She later hired another hitman to finish the
job.

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:18.490
During her 2012 trial, during which she was
free on $1.25 million bail, Haro brought a

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:23.979
huge entourage to the courtroom each day,
including a nun dressed all in white…and

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:27.550
her other ex-husband, Joe Pesci, dressed all
in black.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.870
Haro plead no contest and was sentenced to
just over 12 years in prison.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.560
While during a preliminary hearing, a witness
strongly implied that Pesci had paid for Haro's

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:37.999
hit against Warren.

00:05:37.999 --> 00:05:41.969
Police interviewed him and searched his property
and found him absolutely unrelated to the

00:05:41.969 --> 00:05:42.969
crime.

00:05:42.969 --> 00:05:44.650
Time for a comeback?

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.270
Legal lunacy aside, there's always a chance
Pesci might re-emerge.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:53.199
In the summer of 2016, he made a surprise
appearance at Spike TV's Guys Choice Awards

00:05:53.199 --> 00:05:57.229
alongside De Niro to induct GoodFellas into
the "Guy Movie Hall of Fame."

00:05:57.229 --> 00:06:01.720
De Niro mentioned a long-gestating movie called
The Irishman, which Martin Scorsese had been

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.909
trying to get off the ground for years, but
remained stalled in part because he wanted

00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:09.169
it to star not just De Niro, but the elusive
Pesci.

00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:15.839
"That's if Joe has any more f--- left in him,
so… so far all he keeps saying is go f---

00:06:15.839 --> 00:06:16.839
yourself."

00:06:16.839 --> 00:06:22.740
Finally, in early 2017, Netflix paid $105
million for the global rights to the film,

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.559
and it'll supposedly be available in 2019.

00:06:25.559 --> 00:06:27.720
Could this be the start of Pesci's return?

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.880
Well … maybe.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.780
"You insulted me just a little bit.

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:33.550
Just a little bit, insulted me.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:34.550
You know?

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:35.920
Just a little bit.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.700
It's okay though, I'm used to it."

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:39.899
Thanks for watching!

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.749
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:42.749 --> 00:06:45.089
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

